COMMAND NAME: gpcreatepart.sh

Partitions a table by creating inherited child tables, defining
check constraints, creating rewrite rules, and moving existing data 
from the parent table into the correct child table.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpcreatepart.sh -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p d 
-c {y|m|d|h} -s YYYY-MM-DD -n <number_children> [-m <conversion>] [-r] 
[-b] [-u] [-i <column1>[,<column2>,...]] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-k] [-F] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpcreatepart.sh -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p r 
-s <range_start_value> -e <range_end_value> -g <range_increment> 
[-m <conversion>] [-r] [-b] [-u] [-i <column1>[,<column2>,...]] [-q] 
[-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] [-k] [-F] [-d <master_data_directory>] 
[-x <database>]

gpcreatepart.sh -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p l 
-o <list_value1>,<list_value2>[,<list_value3>,...] [-m <conversion>] [-r] 
[-b] [-u] [-i <column1>[,<column2>,...]] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] [-D] 
[-k] [-F] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpcreatepart.sh -? | -v


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpcreatepart.sh script automates the administrative tasks of 
partitioning a large parent table into several smaller child tables. 
It performs the following tasks:

* Creates child tables that inherit all attributes from the parent table 
  (columns, constraints, distribution key, etc.). Note that permissions 
  and indexes are not inherited. 

* Defines CHECK constraints on the child tables based on the date range, 
  numeric range, or list value specified. The child table names are 
  created using a predefined naming convention of:
  <top_level_parent>_child_depth_<#>_<partition_type>_<data_range>_<type>

* Creates the appropriate rewrite rules on the parent table so that 
  INSERT statements are automatically redirected to the correct child table. 
  The rewrite rules are created using a predefined naming convention of:
  rule_<child_table_name>

* Creates the specified indexes on the child tables. Indexes are created 
  with a predefined naming convention of:
  idx_<column_number>_<child_table_name>

* If the parent table contains data, removes partitioned records from the 
  parent table and inserts them into the correct child table based on 
  the CHECK constraints.

Table partitioning addresses the problem of supporting very large tables, 
such as fact tables, by allowing you to divide them into smaller and more 
manageable pieces. Partitioning is especially useful in data warehouse 
applications, which commonly store and analyze large amounts of 
historical data.

In Greenplum Database, partitioning is a procedure that creates multiple 
sub-tables (or child tables) from a single large table (or parent table). 
The primary purpose is to improve performance by scanning only the relevant 
data needed to satisfy a query. This is done using table inheritance and 
CHECK constraints. Table inheritance creates a persistent relationship 
between a child table and its parent table(s), so that all of the schema 
information from the parent table propagates to its children. By default, 
Greenplum Database scans all of the data in a parent table and all of its 
child tables to satisfy a given query. CHECK constraints limit the data a 
table can contain based on some defining criteria, and are also used by 
the query planner to determine which child tables to scan in order to 
satisfy a given query. 

The way you choose to partition a table depends upon how you plan to 
access the data, and your performance requirements. You should also try to 
choose partitioning criteria that will divide your data as evenly as possible.
A given querys performance improves based on the number of partitions 
created. For example, by dividing a large table into 10 partitions, 
a query will execute 10 times faster than it would against the 
unpartitioned table (provided that the data is equally distributed 
and that the partitions are designed to support the querys criteria).


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <schema>.<parent_table>

Required. The name of the parent table to partition. Table names must 
be qualified with the schema name in which the table resides.


-f <partition_column>

Required. The name of the column that will be evaluated in order to 
partition the parent table.


-p d | r | l (date, range, or list partitions)

Required. Specifies the partitioning type. Greenplum Database supports 
date range partitioning (based on date, timestamp, or timestamp with 
timezone data), numeric range partitioning (based on numeric data), or 
list partitioning (based on character data).


-c y| m | d | h (date range basis)

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies if partitions should be 
created on a yearly, monthly, daily, or hourly basis.


-s <range_start_value>

Required for date and numeric range partitioning. For date partitioning, 
specifies the start date to use as the partitioning starting point in the 
format YYYY-MM-DD. For numeric range partitioning, specifies the numeric 
value to use as the partitioning starting point.


-n <number_of_children> (date range limit)

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies the number of month, day, 
or hour child tables to create from the starting point.


-e <range_end_value> 

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies the range ending 
value for the data contained in the partition column.


-g <range_increment>

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies how to increment 
the records between the starting range and ending range into child tables.


-o <list_values>

Required for list partitioning. A comma separated list of values for 
which to create child tables. Make sure there are no spaces between 
list values and comma separators. To add a null check constraint add 
the text NULL to the list.


-m <conversion>

Optional. The conversion function to used to convert the partition 
column data type format to the format you want to use to evaluate 
CHECK constraints and rewrite rules. For example, if a numeric column 
contains timestamp data in the format 1138576193.697, supplying 
-m to_timestamp will convert the contents of the column to a timestamp format.


-r (no rewrite rules)

Does not create rewrite rules to redirect INSERT statements on the 
parent table to the correct child table. The default is to create 
INSERT rewrite rules on the parent table.


-b (show child row counts)

Shows the number of records in each child table. This is only relevant if 
the parent table contained data prior to being partitioned.


-u (index partition column)

Creates an index on each of the child tables for the partition column 
declared with the -f option.


-i <additional_index_columns>

Creates an index on each of the child tables for the specified column(s).


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l <log_directory>

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By default 
log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output to debug level.


-k (keep temp files)

Keeps temporary files to use for diagnostics.


-F (force)

Forces child table creation when the parent already has child tables.


-x <database_name>

Optional. The name of the database where the parent table resides. 
If not supplied, the script uses the default database 
(which is the most recently created database).


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.



*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Partition the hr.employee table based on salary range. By looking at the 
data, you have determined the lowest salary value to be 10000 and the 
highest to be 400000. You want the child tables to be incremented in 
the ranges of 20000.

gpcreatepart.sh -t hr.employee -f salary -p r -s 10000 -e 400000 -g 20000


Partition the baby.names table based on gender. This will divide the 
table into two child tables, one for males and one for females. You also 
want the child tables to be indexed on the name_id column.

gpcreatepart.sh -t baby.names -f gender -p l -o M,F -i name_id


Partition the sales.sales table based on date range. Create one child 
table per month for the past 12 months of data. The sale_date column you 
are using for partitioning is currently in numeric format, so you need 
to convert it to a timestamp format in order to partition it by date.

gpcreatepart.sh -t sales.sales -f sale_date -p d -c m -s 
2005-11-01 -n 12 -m to_timestamp


